// Tutorial 37: Panics — run-time errors
//
// Run it:      cabby 37-panics.cby
//
// Most mistakes are caught before a Cabby program runs: type errors, using
// a moved value, conflicting borrows. A few can only be noticed while it
// runs. Then the program PANICS: it stops at once, prints where and why,
// and exits with status 101. It never carries on with corrupted memory.
//
// What panics:
//   - an array index out of bounds
//   - dividing (or taking %) by zero, and INT_MIN / -1
//   - shifting by a negative amount or by the type's width or more
//   - recursing too deeply (stack overflow)
//
// What does NOT panic (it's defined behavior, as listed in tutorials 3-4):
//   - integer overflow wraps around
//   - float division by zero gives inf or NaN
//   - a float-to-integer cast saturates
//
// No destructors run after a panic: the program just stops.
//
// This program runs until it reads past the end of an array. Uncomment
// the other lines one at a time to see each kind of panic.

// (For Cabby's test suite, which checks every tutorial: this one is
// expected to end with the panic below.)
// panic: index 4 is out of bounds for an array of length 4

int depth int n {
	return depth(n + 1) + 1        // never stops: a stack overflow
}

int main {
	int Top = 2147483647
	Top++
	println "overflow wraps:", Top
	println "float division by zero:", 1.0 / 0.0

	int Zero = 0
	// println 10 / Zero
	// println 1 << (Zero + 40)
	// println depth(0)

	int Squares[4] = {0, 1, 4, 9}
	for i in 0 to 4 {      // `to` includes 4: one too far
		println i, "squared is", Squares[i]
	}
	println "never printed"
}

// In the interactive session (run `cabby` on its own), a panic only stops
// the line you typed; the session carries on.
//
// Try this:
//  - Fix the loop so it stops at the right place.
//  - Uncomment each panic in turn and read the message.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- output
//> overflow wraps: -2147483648
//> float division by zero: inf
//> 0 squared is 0
//> 1 squared is 1
//> 2 squared is 4
//> 3 squared is 9
